(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-8-hematologic-malignancies-ii-vignettes-version-2"]={"title":"Hematologic Malignancies II Vignettes 2 — CMS I Exam 8","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 8","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 34-year-old woman being treated for laboratory-confirmed pertussis has an absolute lymphocyte count of 7,900 per microliter (normal 1,000-4,000) with atypical lymphocytes on the smear. She has no lymphadenopathy or organomegaly. What is the best interpretation of her lymphocytosis?","choices":["Monoclonal B lymphocytosis","Burkitt lymphoma","Reactive infectious lymphocytosis","Chronic lymphocytic leukemia"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Atypical lymphocytosis can be seen with some infections, including pertussis, which is on the differential of chronic lymphocytic leukemia. Her proven infection, young age (that leukemia has a median age of 70) and clean examination fit a reactive cause. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"A 77-year-old woman with chronic lymphocytic leukemia confirmed by flow cytometry reports fatigue. Her absolute lymphocyte count is 56,000 per microliter (normal 1,000-4,000). She has axillary lymphadenopathy and splenomegaly. Hemoglobin is 9.4 g/dL (normal 12.0-15.5 g/dL) and platelets 182,000 per microliter (normal 150,000-450,000). What is her Rai stage?","choices":["Stage I","Stage II","Stage III","Stage IV"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Item by item: lymphocytosis yes (56,000); lymphadenopathy and splenomegaly yes; anemia yes (9.4 g/dL, below 12.0); thrombocytopenia no (182,000). Lymphocytosis with anemia is stage III, with or without nodes and organomegaly, so the anemia sets the stage. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"A 72-year-old man has an absolute lymphocyte count of 25,000 per microliter (normal 1,000-4,000) for 4 months and no symptoms. Flow cytometry is sent on his peripheral blood. Which result supports chronic lymphocytic leukemia?","choices":["Binucleated Reed-Sternberg cells","Polyclonal reactive T cells","Clonal plasma cells making IgG","A clonal B-cell population"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Flow cytometry confirms chronic lymphocytic leukemia by identifying a clonal B-cell population with coexpression of CD19 (cluster of differentiation 19) and CD5 (cluster of differentiation 5) on the lymphocytes. With his count above 5,000 for 3 months, that confirms it. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man has monoclonal B lymphocytosis: an absolute lymphocyte count of 4,300 per microliter (normal 1,000-4,000) with a clonal B-cell population and no lymphadenopathy, organomegaly, cytopenias or symptoms. He asks how often this progresses to chronic lymphocytic leukemia. Which rate is accurate?","choices":["About 1-2% per year","About 10% per year","About 25% per year","About 50% per year"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Monoclonal B lymphocytosis progresses to chronic lymphocytic leukemia at a rate of about 1-2% per year. His count below 5,000 with no nodes, organomegaly, cytopenias or symptoms keeps him in the precursor state for now. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slides 9, 57"},{"q":"A 70-year-old woman's primary care clinician finds an absolute lymphocyte count of 16,000 per microliter (normal 1,000-4,000), and flow cytometry confirms chronic lymphocytic leukemia. She has no symptoms. Which referral is appropriate?","choices":["No referral is needed","Hematology/oncology","Infectious disease","Surgical oncology"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Chronic lymphocytic leukemia requires referral to, and management by, a hematologist/oncologist, even when asymptomatic and observed. Treatment choice depends on patient preferences and mutations, and follow-up includes lab monitoring. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man has classical Hodgkin lymphoma. Staging imaging shows involved left cervical lymph nodes and right inguinal lymph nodes, with no liver, bone marrow or other organ involvement. What is his stage?","choices":["Stage 1","Stage 2","Stage 3","Stage 4"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Cervical nodes are above the diaphragm and inguinal nodes below it; with no organ involvement, two or more node regions above and below the diaphragm is stage 3. His age over 60 is the second peak of Hodgkin lymphoma's bimodal incidence. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"A 46-year-old man has a 3-cm firm, painless left axillary lymph node that has grown over 6 weeks. There is no infection to explain it. Which test establishes the diagnosis?","choices":["Flow cytometry of blood","Lactate dehydrogenase level","Excisional lymph node biopsy","Fine-needle aspiration"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Lymphoma is diagnosed by excisional lymph node biopsy, reserved for significant enlargement (2 cm), persistent and progressively enlarging nodes; his 3-cm growing node qualifies. Excision shows the node architecture needed for the diagnosis. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slides 8, 25"},{"q":"A 52-year-old woman has diffuse large B-cell lymphoma involving cervical lymph nodes, the liver and the bone marrow. Her ECOG (Eastern Cooperative Oncology Group) performance status is 1, and her lactate dehydrogenase is normal. How many poor-prognosis factors does she have?","choices":["One factor","Two factors","Three factors","Four factors"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Item by item: age over 60, no (52); extranodal sites, yes (liver and marrow); ECOG (Eastern Cooperative Oncology Group) status above 2, no (1); stage III or IV, yes (multiple organs is stage 4); elevated lactate dehydrogenase, no. Total 2. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slides 26, 30"},{"q":"A 75-year-old woman with chronic lymphocytic leukemia has been observed for 2 years. She has no symptoms, but her absolute lymphocyte count has risen from 18,000 to 41,000 per microliter (normal 1,000-4,000) over 4 months. What is the next step?","choices":["Begin targeted therapy","Continue watch and wait","Repeat the count in a year","Obtain a bone marrow biopsy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Observation lasts until rapid progression or disease-related symptoms. Her count has more than doubled in 4 months, under the 6-month mark, which is rapid progression and an indication to treat, with targeted therapies such as a Bruton tyrosine kinase inhibitor. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slides 8, 11"},{"q":"A 72-year-old Black man has 2 months of back and pelvic pain, constipation, increased thirst, urinary frequency, fatigue and ankle swelling. Calcium is 11.9 mg/dL (normal 8.5-10.5 mg/dL), hemoglobin 9.6 g/dL (normal 13.5-17.5 g/dL) and creatinine 1.9 mg/dL (normal 0.6-1.2 mg/dL); skull x-ray shows multiple punched-out lytic lesions. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Hodgkin lymphoma","Multiple myeloma","Waldenstrom macroglobulinemia","Chronic lymphocytic leukemia"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. His findings are the CRAB (calcium, renal, anemia, bone) features: hypercalcemia (constipation, thirst, urinary frequency), renal insufficiency (swelling), anemia (fatigue) and bone pain with punched-out osteolytic lesions. Age over 65 and African American race raise myeloma risk. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slides 46, 48"},{"q":"A 22-year-old man in the United States has an abdominal mass that has visibly enlarged over 24 hours. Biopsy shows a starry sky pattern (sheets of lymphoma cells with benign macrophages clearing dying cells), consistent with sporadic Burkitt lymphoma. Which referral is appropriate?","choices":["Emergent hematology referral","Routine oncology visit in 4 weeks","Surgery to remove the mass first","Repeat imaging in 3 months"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Burkitt lymphoma's doubling time is extremely fast, with tumor expanding within 24 hours, so treatment must start promptly with emergent referral to a hematologist and rapid transfer of care. Early multi-agent chemotherapy gives an excellent prognosis. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slides 32, 33"},{"q":"A 69-year-old woman has nodal marginal zone lymphoma, a low-grade B-cell lymphoma, involving several node regions above the diaphragm. She has developed bothersome symptoms and now needs treatment, but her disease is not extensive. Which agent is used?","choices":["Rituximab","Cyclophosphamide, doxorubicin, vincristine, prednisone","Doxorubicin, bleomycin, vinblastine, dacarbazine","Allopurinol alone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Low-grade B-cell lymphomas are treated with rituximab (an anti-CD20 (cluster of differentiation 20) monoclonal antibody) when treatment is required, with chemotherapy added if the disease is extensive. Hers needs treatment but is not extensive. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slides 27, 41"},{"q":"A 34-year-old woman with interstitial lung disease has newly diagnosed classical Hodgkin lymphoma. The historical regimen of doxorubicin, bleomycin, vinblastine and dacarbazine is being considered. Which drug in it is the concern because of her lungs?","choices":["Vinblastine","Dacarbazine","Bleomycin","Doxorubicin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Bleomycin carries pulmonary toxicities, which is why newer agents are replacing it in Hodgkin lymphoma regimens. With pre-existing interstitial lung disease she is the patient in whom bleomycin is most worrying. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"A 63-year-old woman has an M-protein of 3.9 g/dL, and bone marrow biopsy shows 24% clonal plasma cells. She has no symptoms; calcium, creatinine and hemoglobin are normal, and whole-body imaging shows no bone lesions. How should her plasma cell disorder be classified?","choices":["Monoclonal gammopathy of undetermined significance","Multiple myeloma","Reactive hypergammaglobulinemia of cirrhosis","Smoldering multiple myeloma"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Reading the criteria: M-protein above 3 g/dL (3.9) and marrow plasma cells within 10-60% (24%), with no myeloma-defining events (no anemia, bone lesions, renal failure or hypercalcemia). That is smoldering myeloma, an intermediate stage that typically does not require treatment. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slides 56, 57"},{"q":"A 69-year-old woman with chronic lymphocytic leukemia now needs treatment for symptomatic disease. She prefers a time-limited course rather than taking pills indefinitely, even if it is more intensive. Which approach fits her preference?","choices":["Venetoclax with obinutuzumab","Ibrutinib monotherapy","Acalabrutinib monotherapy","Observation alone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A BCL-2 (B-cell lymphoma 2) inhibitor, venetoclax, is combined with a monoclonal antibody such as obinutuzumab for a fixed duration (infusion plus pill, time limited but more intensive). Bruton tyrosine kinase inhibitors are usually continuous as monotherapy. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slides 11, 13"},{"q":"A 64-year-old woman has newly diagnosed symptomatic multiple myeloma. Which initial regimen is used?","choices":["Proteasome inhibitor, immunomodulatory drug and steroid","Rituximab with cyclophosphamide and doxorubicin","Doxorubicin, bleomycin, vinblastine and dacarbazine","Venetoclax with obinutuzumab for a fixed duration"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Newly diagnosed patients are treated with a 3- or 4-drug regimen with a proteasome inhibitor and immunomodulatory drug backbone with steroids. The goal is prolonged remission to improve survival and quality of life, as myeloma is incurable. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"A 22-year-old man has biopsy-proven classical Hodgkin lymphoma presenting as a painless cervical node. Which imaging study in the initial work-up checks for a mediastinal mass or adenopathy?","choices":["Lumbar puncture","Upper endoscopy","Skull x-ray","Chest x-ray"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A chest x-ray rules out a mediastinal mass or adenopathy, which is common in Hodgkin lymphoma. Work-up also includes a complete blood count, chemistries (electrolytes, for tumor lysis), lactate dehydrogenase and bone marrow biopsy. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slides 25, 39"},{"q":"A 71-year-old farmer with years of pesticide exposure has blurred vision and headaches. Serum studies show a large IgM monoclonal protein, and he has signs of hyperviscosity; there are no lytic bone lesions or hypercalcemia. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Smoldering myeloma","Chronic lymphocytic leukemia","Waldenstrom macroglobulinemia","Multiple myeloma"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Lymphoplasmacytic lymphoma (Waldenstrom macroglobulinemia) causes excess IgM, a large antibody that causes hyperviscosity with blurred vision and headache. Farming and pesticides are listed risk factors. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slides 18, 49"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man has peripheral T-cell lymphoma, not otherwise specified, and is to start a CHOP (cyclophosphamide, doxorubicin, vincristine, prednisone)-type regimen. Which drug used with the B-cell version of this regimen should be left out?","choices":["Prednisone","Rituximab","Cyclophosphamide","Doxorubicin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. T-cell lymphomas are treated with similar regimens (CHOP (cyclophosphamide, doxorubicin, vincristine, prednisone)) minus rituximab, because rituximab is an anti-CD20 (cluster of differentiation 20) antibody and T cells do not express CD20 (cluster of differentiation 20). — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A 72-year-old woman has newly diagnosed multiple myeloma. Her beta-2 microglobulin is 6.8 mg/L (normal 0.8-2.2 mg/L), albumin 3.9 g/dL (normal 3.5-5.0 g/dL), chromosomal studies show deletion 17p, and lactate dehydrogenase is above the upper limit of normal. What is her R-ISS (Revised International Staging System) stage?","choices":["Stage I","Stage II","Stage III","Stage IV"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. R-ISS (Revised International Staging System) stage III: beta-2 microglobulin at least 5.5 mg/L regardless of albumin (6.8), high-risk chromosomal abnormalities (deletion 17p), and high lactate dehydrogenase. She meets all three, so her normal albumin does not matter. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"A 72-year-old man has diffuse large B-cell lymphoma with involved axillary and inguinal lymph nodes and no extranodal sites. He spends most of the day in a chair, with an ECOG (Eastern Cooperative Oncology Group) performance status of 3, and his lactate dehydrogenase is elevated. How many poor-prognosis factors does he have?","choices":["One factor","Two factors","Three factors","Four factors"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Item by item: age over 60, yes (72); extranodal sites, no; ECOG (Eastern Cooperative Oncology Group) status above 2, yes (3); stage III or IV, yes (axillary nodes above and inguinal nodes below the diaphragm, stage III); elevated lactate dehydrogenase, yes. Total 4. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slides 26, 30"},{"q":"A 30-year-old woman with aggressive lymphoma develops tumor lysis syndrome on day 2 of chemotherapy, with rising potassium, phosphorus, uric acid and creatinine. Which consequence of this syndrome carries the largest risk?","choices":["Acute renal failure","Hypercalcemic constipation","Hyperviscosity stroke","Hyperkalemic arrhythmia"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The largest risk of tumor lysis syndrome is hyperkalemia leading to arrhythmia and potential death. Acute renal failure from uric acid and calcium phosphate crystals also occurs and worsens the condition, but the potassium is the killer. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"A 7-year-old boy has Burkitt lymphoma that was identified early, and he is starting multi-agent chemotherapy promptly. His parents ask about his long-term outlook. Which long-term survival figure applies to children treated this way?","choices":["Under 10%","About 25%","50-90%","Above 90%"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. With early identification and multi-agent chemotherapy, Burkitt lymphoma has an excellent long-term prognosis: above 90% in children and 50-90% in adults. Prompt treatment, with care for tumor lysis, is what earns that outcome. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man with multiple myeloma has responded to initial 3-drug therapy and is a transplant candidate. Which plan has been shown to improve overall survival?","choices":["Stop treatment once in remission","Radiation to the spine alone","Watch and wait for relapse","Autologous transplant, then maintenance"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. For a transplant candidate, autologous transplant has been shown to improve overall survival, as has maintenance therapy after transplant. Most patients still relapse and need continuous therapy for disease control. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"A 70-year-old woman has back pain, a hemoglobin of 9.8 g/dL (normal 12.0-15.5 g/dL) and a calcium of 11.4 mg/dL (normal 8.5-10.5 mg/dL). Multiple myeloma is suspected. Which blood test shows the characteristic finding?","choices":["Uric acid level","Serum protein electrophoresis","Flow cytometry of blood","Lactate dehydrogenase"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A monoclonal protein on serum protein electrophoresis is characteristic of myeloma, with a positive urine monoclonal protein (Bence Jones protein) on 24-hour urine electrophoresis. Work-up adds light chain ratio, bone marrow biopsy with flow, and imaging. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"A 55-year-old woman has follicular lymphoma. Positron emission tomography shows involved inguinal lymph nodes and para-aortic lymph nodes in the abdomen; nothing is involved above the diaphragm and no organ is involved. What is her stage?","choices":["Stage 1","Stage 2","Stage 3","Stage 4"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Two node regions (inguinal and para-aortic), both below the diaphragm, with no organ involvement. Stage 2 is two or more lymph node regions on the same side of the diaphragm, and the same side can be the lower side. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 61-year-old woman has had painless lymph nodes in the neck and groin that have waxed and waned for 2 years. Excisional biopsy shows follicular lymphoma with a t(14;18) translocation. She has no symptoms. What is the most appropriate management now?","choices":["Watch and wait","Multi-drug chemotherapy now","Allogeneic stem cell transplant","Emergent hospital admission"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Follicular lymphoma, the most common indolent non-Hodgkin lymphoma, frequently needs no treatment (watch and wait); intermittent low-intensity therapy is used if symptomatic. She is asymptomatic, so observation fits. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slides 16, 27, 34"},{"q":"A 67-year-old woman has classical Hodgkin lymphoma of the mixed cellularity subtype, stage II and not bulky. Which features of her case predict inferior results?","choices":["Her stage and subtype","Her age and bulky disease","Her age and subtype","Her sex and stage"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Hodgkin lymphoma is generally curable, but inferior results are seen in older patients, those with bulky disease and those with certain subtypes (lymphocyte depleted or mixed cellularity). She is older and has mixed cellularity; her disease is not bulky. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slides 41, 42"},{"q":"A 9-year-old boy from sub-Saharan Africa has a painless jaw swelling that his mother says grew visibly over a single day. He has fever, night sweats and weight loss, and the tumor cells test positive for Epstein-Barr virus. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Burkitt lymphoma","Hodgkin lymphoma","Diffuse large B-cell lymphoma","Follicular lymphoma"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Burkitt lymphoma is a very aggressive lymphoma with very rapidly growing, often painless swelling (changes visible within a day) and B symptoms (fever, night sweats, weight loss). The endemic type is seen in sub-Saharan Africa and strongly associated with Epstein-Barr virus. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slides 31, 32"},{"q":"A 63-year-old man has biopsy-proven non-Hodgkin lymphoma. Staging imaging shows involved lymph nodes in the left cervical region and the left axillary region; there are no nodes below the diaphragm and no organ involvement. What is his stage?","choices":["Stage 1","Stage 2","Stage 3","Stage 4"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Reading the node map: two node regions (cervical and axillary), both above the diaphragm, with no organ involvement. Two or more lymph node regions on the same side of the diaphragm is stage 2. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slide 26"}]};
